## Decoding the question
Three parts. **"Define"** — give a precise, sourced definition. **"Explain various forms… in Pakistan"** — classify and illustrate with Pakistani **data and cases**. **"Devise practical strategies"** — design a plan that can be implemented, with concrete measures and priorities. Avoid repeating 2016 and 2017 answers word for word: here the emphasis is on **definition** and **practicality**.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition in brief
**Gender-based violence (GBV)** is harm inflicted on a person **because of their gender**, arising from unequal power between women and men.

#### ii) The argument
In Pakistan GBV takes physical, sexual, psychological, economic and custom-based forms, at home, in the community, at work and online. Because these forms share one root — men's **power and control**, protected by **impunity** — practical strategies must begin with **justice and services**, which can be delivered now through existing laws, and build towards long-term **prevention** that changes norms.

### 2. Definition
The UN **Declaration on the Elimination of Violence against Women** (==1993==) defines violence against women as "any act of gender-based violence that results in, or is likely to result in, **physical, sexual or psychological harm or suffering** to women, including **threats** of such acts, **coercion** or **arbitrary deprivation of liberty**, whether occurring in **public or in private life**". In Pakistan, the **Punjab Protection of Women against Violence Act 2016** defines violence to include **domestic violence, sexual violence, psychological abuse, economic abuse, stalking and cybercrime**. Worldwide, about **1 in 3** women experience physical or sexual violence (WHO, ==2021==).

### 3. Theory
Feminist theory explains GBV as a means of **power and control**: the **Duluth Power and Control Wheel** (==1981==) shows how intimidation, isolation, economic abuse and threats, backed by physical violence, keep women subordinate. **Social learning theory** shows that violence is **learned** and continues when unpunished. **Lori Heise**'s **ecological model** (==1998==) places causes at four levels — individual, relationship, community and society — and so shows where strategies must act.

### 4. Forms 1 — physical violence

#### i) Domestic violence
The **PDHS 2017–18** found that **28%** of ever-married women had experienced physical violence since age 15; **34%** had experienced spousal physical, sexual or emotional violence.

#### ii) Acid attacks
Acid is thrown on women who refuse proposals or advances, blinding and disfiguring them.

#### iii) Honour killing
At least **405** honour killings were recorded in ==2024== (HRCP), and the **SSDO** counted **547** with a conviction rate of about **0.5%**.

### 5. Forms 2 — sexual violence

#### i) Rape
**5,339** rapes were reported in ==2024==. High-profile cases — the rape and murder of **Zainab Ansari** in Kasur (==2018==) and the **motorway gang rape** (==2020==) — led to the **Zainab Alert Act 2020** and the **Anti-Rape (Investigation and Trial) Act 2021**.

#### ii) Harassment
Sexual harassment at work, in universities, on transport and online is widespread despite the **2010** workplace law and **Section 509 PPC**.

#### iii) Child and forced marriage
Marriage before 18, or without consent, exposes girls to sexual violence and ends their education; only **Sindh** (==2013==) and **Islamabad** (==2025==) have fixed the minimum age at 18.

### 6. Forms 3 — psychological and economic violence

#### i) Psychological violence
Threats of divorce, insults, isolation from family, confiscation of phones and constant surveillance cause depression and sometimes suicide.

#### ii) Economic violence
Taking a woman's earnings or dowry, forbidding her to work, and denying her **inheritance** keep her dependent — and dependence traps her in violence.

### 7. Forms 4 — custom-based, cyber and state violence

#### i) Custom-based
***Vani*** and ***swara***, **marriage to the Qur'an** and forced marriage, often ordered by *jirgas*, were criminalised by the **Prevention of Anti-Women Practices Act 2011**.

#### ii) Cyber violence
Blackmail with private images and online harassment are covered by **PECA 2016**; online visibility has also triggered killings — of **Qandeel Baloch** (==2016==) and the 17-year-old **Sana Yousaf** (==2025==).

#### iii) State violence
Custodial abuse and police refusal to register cases make the state itself a site of violence.

::: fig grid | The forms of violence against women in Pakistan.
@cols 3
Physical: domestic beating, acid attacks, honour killing, stove burning
Sexual: rape, harassment, child and forced marriage
Psychological: threats, isolation, humiliation, surveillance
Economic: taking income, denying work and inheritance
Custom-based: vani, swara, marriage to the Qur'an
Cyber and state: online blackmail; custodial abuse; police inaction
:::

### 8. Why practical strategies must target causes
A strategy is practical only if it addresses why violence continues. The evidence points to three causes: **patriarchal norms** that justify control, women's **economic dependence**, and **impunity** — conviction rates of about **0.5%** for rape and honour killing in ==2024== (SSDO). Pakistan already has laws against almost every form above; the practical task is to make them **work**.

### 9. Practical strategy 1: justice

#### i) Registration and investigation
Make FIR registration compulsory, with penalties for officers who refuse; recruit more **women police**; fully staff the **Special Sexual Offences Investigation Units** created by the 2021 Act.

#### ii) Evidence and courts
Build **DNA laboratories** and train women medico-legal officers; extend **special courts** with fast, in-camera trials; protect witnesses.

#### iii) No pardons
Make honour killings and other GBV crimes fully **non-compoundable**, and prosecute *jirga* members who order violence.

### 10. Practical strategy 2: services
Replicate the **Violence against Women Centre, Multan** (==2017==) — where a woman can register a case, receive medical examination, legal aid and counselling in one place — in every district. Keep helplines such as **1043** staffed around the clock; make **shelters** safe and dignified; enforce **protection, residence and monetary orders** under the Punjab Act 2016 and other provincial laws. Use **Lady Health Workers**, who visit homes, to identify and refer survivors.

### 11. Practical strategy 3: prevention

#### i) Schools
Teach respect, consent and non-violent conflict resolution; keep girls in school, which delays marriage.

#### ii) Scholars and media
Ask religious scholars to declare in Friday sermons that honour killing is murder and that forced marriage and denial of inheritance are un-Islamic; use television dramas and the **16 Days of Activism** to model respectful relationships.

#### iii) Men and boys
Expand programmes, like those of **Rozan**, that challenge the idea that violence proves manhood.

#### iv) Economic power
Enforce the **2020** property law, expand women's jobs and cash transfers through **BISP**, and set the minimum marriage age at 18 everywhere.

::: fig pyramid | Practical strategies in order of priority — quick wins at the top, long-term change at the base.
Now: compulsory FIRs, special courts, helplines, one-stop centres in every district
Next: forensic capacity, women police, protection orders enforced, shelters improved
Ongoing: schools, scholars, media and men's programmes to change norms
Foundation: women's education, property and income; marriage at 18
:::

### 12. Priorities and conclusion

#### i) Priorities
Begin with **justice and services** — they use existing laws and staff and can show results quickly. Build **prevention** in parallel, because it takes a generation. Measure progress through a **national GBV database** that publishes cases and convictions every year.

#### ii) Conclusion
Gender-based violence is harm rooted in unequal gender power. In Pakistan it takes physical, sexual, psychological, economic, custom-based and cyber forms. The most practical strategy is to end **impunity** through effective policing and courts, to bring **services** within every woman's reach, and to change norms through schools, mosques, the media and men themselves — while giving women the **economic independence** that makes resistance possible. Pakistan has the laws; it now needs the will to enforce them.

## Why this answer scores
- Gives a **sourced definition** (DEVAW 1993; Punjab Act 2016) and a theoretical frame.
- Explains the forms **by category** with up-to-date **data and cases**.
- Strategies are genuinely **practical** — prioritised, costed in existing institutions, and measurable.
- A **grid** and a **pyramid** figure organise forms and priorities.
